speechtext:
I suppose I am included in the indictment. The matter was brought to my attention and not only received consideration but decision. I will give the ground of the decision to my hon. friend. I do not know whether it got very long attention at the hands of the present Minister of Trade and Commerce; I am inclined to think this is about the first he has heard of it. The act was passed in order to make oil production in Ontario a business possibility. The Ontario lands were granted in fee simple, the oil exploiter there had to buy his land or his right and parliament considered that unless this bounty were granted the capital investment necessary was such, and the production possibilities so low, that there would not be any oil production. It has been continued for that reason. Very careful examination has disclosed that it has been necessary-whether it is just now I could not say, but I fancy so-or there would not be oil production. So much for the Ontario situation. In the West we own the lands and we make leases. The exploiter pays a certain rental per acre the first year and a smaller rental thereafter, and such moneys as he puts into actual development are credited against the rental for two years- I am disposed to think I extended that to three. All these things do not apply in Ontario. In western Canada the capital expenditures that the oil exploiter puts in are credited against this rental; when he gets oil he then proceeds to pay rental based on his oil production. What would be the common sense of charging the oil well owner a rental on his oil production and then paying him a bounty on that same production? If the rent is too high, make it less, but I cannot see any object in taking a rental from him and putting it into the treasury and then taking it out again- and paying it back to him in the form of bonus. The point is that the system of production in the two places is wholly different. The system in the West credits the oil man with part of his capital investment;
1G0
when he gets oil we then charge him rental, because there we are the owners of the property not the private individual. In Ontario the private individual is the owner. The man who goes in to produce oil, makes his capital investment and then he may have to pay rental-royalty, we call it-to the owner of the property. He does not pay anything here; if he did it would be senseless for us in Ontario to pay him back something by way of bonus.
